  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unseasonable warmth and dry weather continues\ntoday \n\nNot much change to the overall synoptic pattern, High pressure \nover the North Atlantic will maintain steady south to southwest\nwinds. Will continue to favor the warmest temperature guidance\ntoday since temperatures did not lower much overnight. Both the\nREFS and NBM had rather high probabilities for max temperatures\nto be 15-20 degrees above normal today, especially across\neastern MA as well as the CT River valley. Some record high\ntemperatures could be met or broken. Record at BOS is 78, PVD \n77, ORH 74, BDL 78. \n\nA warm day will be followed by another warm night. Overnight\ntemperatures fall into the 50s which is about average for our \nhigh temperatures this time of year. Some locations may have a \nshot at meeting/breaking the high min record. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Wet and unsettled weather returns Thursday into \nthis weekend. Continued above normal temperatures before \ncooler conditions arrive Sunday and beyond.\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to herald the end of our highly\nanomalous stretch of dry weather by Thursday, kicking off a \nperiod of 36-48 hours of periodic rainfall, sometimes heavy. \nThis comes courtesy of a few things; first is an approaching \nfront extending from a trough of low pressure crossing the Great\nLakes. After moving through NY state, this front stalls across \nNew England from SW to NE late Thursday. This brings an initial\nround of rain chances for Thursday, mainly for western areas of\nSNE, while much of the region southeast of a BDL-ORH line may\nremain dry. A more widespread and heavier rainfall should then \narrive for Friday into early Saturday as a secondary low\npressure forms along this front. It deepens as it lifts north \nin the vicinity of Cape Cod Saturday, and then even moreso over\nthe Gulf of Maine on Sunday, by which time we'll be on the \ndrier side. \n\nThere will be a lot of moisture for this system to work with as \npersistent moist SW flow ahead of the upper trough pulls in PWATs \nover 1.5\" and dewpoints into the lower 60s! This will lead to \nvery warm conditions Friday morning (in the lower 60s) but more\nimportantly, fuel for the good dynamics to work with. These \nelevated dewpoints will generate some instability, allowing for \nsome convection to contribute to localized downpours, while \nconvergence around the low and placement beneath the RRQ of a \n105kt upper jet will provide more lift in the meso/synoptic \nscale.\n\nThe exact placement of the swath of heaviest rainfall will\nunfortunately not be nailed down until we get closer to this\nevent as it will be determined by where exactly this front \nstalls. Generally ensemble guidance is calling for a 1-3\"\nrainfall, with EPS and GEFS calling for a 50-60% and 30-40% chance\nof 1\" or greater rainfall respectively, centered over central\nand eastern SNE. Fortunately, we've been very dry and flash \nflood guidance is high. Thus, not expecting flash flooding to \nbe an issue though some poor drainage/urban flooding is possible\nespecially given the leaves blocking drainage this time of \nyear. There is a marginal risk within the Excessive Rainfall\nOutlook for Friday into Saturday.\n\nBeyond Saturday, we'll see some scattered showers continuing as\nthe upper trough is slow to exit overhead; nothing like \nFriday/Saturday though. NW flow ushers in a colder, albeit\ncloser to normal, airmass for early next week with high\ntemperatures returning to the 50s.",
